Install with cp rather than cp -p, so installed modes are the target's.

WHY: libvpx's install rule uses `cp -p`, which preserves the SOURCE file's
ownership, permissions and timestamps rather than applying the install target's.
BLFS carries the same edit and explains it as correcting "the ownership and
permissions of installed files".

IT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN THERE. Every path in the image is recorded in
files.tsv with its mode, and `veron compare` checks that record against a
running system. A library whose permissions came from whatever the tarball
happened to carry is a mode nobody chose, and it would differ between a build
from a fresh unpack and one from a restored checkpoint.

WHY A PATCH RATHER THAN A sed STEP. This was first written as
`sed -i 's/cp -p/cp/' build/make/Makefile` inside a shell step, and that was
the only place in the set where source was modified outside patches/ --
ROADMAP.md makes the recipe the unit and the other four source edits (glib,
graphite2, wlroots twice) are all patches. apply_patches records a patch in
PLAN.txt by name and FAILS THE BUILD if it no longer applies; a sed that
matches nothing succeeds silently and ships an unmodified file, which is
exactly the failure this project keeps finding elsewhere.

Generated with `diff -u`, not written by hand.

--- a/build/make/Makefile
+++ b/build/make/Makefile
@@ -302,7 +302,7 @@
 $(DIST_DIR)/$(1): $(2)
 	$(if $(quiet),@echo "    [INSTALL] $$@")
 	$(qexec)mkdir -p $$(dir $$@)
-	$(qexec)cp -p $$< $$@
+	$(qexec)cp $$< $$@
 endef
 
